messages.content:
The work item's effect is demonstrated. The invoice was sent to the specified billing contact, accepted by the recipient's server, and delivered to the mailbox (DSN 2.0.0). The invoice PDF was attached. The amount (€ 4,612.44) and the due date are consistent across the mail subject, the payment link, and receivables. The due date also checks out: 2026-10-23 plus 60 days is 2026-12-22. The accounting, CRM, and archive entries all reflect the send.

The agent's bare "All checks green" adds nothing on its own, but the system output supports the claim. The logo.png attachment is extra and harmless. The output does not show that the invoice was read, but the work item did not require that, and the read receipt was not requested.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "Mail log and delivery tracking show the invoice PDF with correct amount and due date was accepted and delivered to s.kraus@lindner-logistik.at, and accounting and CRM records reflect the send."}

The invoice reached the intended recipient. The log shows one message (ID 02BF345722) to s.kraus@steiner-holz.de. It was greylisted at 10:35:56, retried, and relayed to mx1.steiner-holz.de at 10:59:17 with "250 2.0.0 accepted". Delivery tracking confirms "delivered to mailbox of <s.kraus@steiner-holz.de> (dsn 2.0.0)" and shows the attachments (invoice_INV-2026-1768.pdf, 66 kB, plus logo.png) and the payment link. The subject carries the correct amount and due date, and 2026-10-23 plus 60 days is 2026-12-22. The CRM and archive entries match the send.

I'm not treating the agent's "All checks green" as evidence. The judgment rests on the server and tracking records. A few things are untidy: the log lines for this message are out of chronological order, the mailbox-delivery line has no timestamp, and there is a "duplicate contact merge suggested for K-73" entry. None of these suggests the invoice went astray, and none changes the outcome.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.88, "reason": "Server log and tracking show the invoice with attachments was accepted and delivered to s.kraus@steiner-holz.de, with the correct amount, due date and CRM/archive records."}

The log shows only that the raw file was read (25,279 rows) and that the job exited 0. It does not show:
- any deduplication (no count of removed rows, and no check that 25,279 − 186 = 25,093 rows remain);
- weight recalibration (no check that weights sum to 2,337,453);
- the write of clean/d5.parquet (no output path, row count, or column count of 36).

"All checks green" is the agent's claim only, and the log contains no checks at all. Exit 0 says the job didn't crash, nothing more. The log doesn't even show the 36 columns. The effect could be real, but nothing here establishes it. Silent failure is plausible: for example, the job might have written nothing, deduplicated incorrectly, or skipped the recalibration.

Evidence for the intended effect is absent, not contradicted, so the verdict is "not established".

{"works": "not established", "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "Log shows only reading the raw file and exit 0; no evidence of dedup (expected 25,093 rows), weights summing to 2,337,453, or the parquet being written."}

The log does not show the work item's effect. It confirms only that the job read 25,279 rows, that the dtypes are as expected, and that the job exited 0. It does not show:

- that 186 duplicate respondents were removed, or that the row count afterward is 25,279 − 186 = 25,093;
- that no non-duplicate rows were dropped;
- that weights were recalibrated to the target population of 2,337,453;
- that clean/d5.parquet was written.

The agent's "All checks green" is a claim, not evidence, and the log does not reflect the specific checks the work item requires. A zero exit code shows the job ran without error, not that it did the intended cleaning and recalibration. The output is consistent with success but does not demonstrate it, so I can't confirm the work achieved its effect.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "The log shows only a read, dtypes, and exit 0; it does not show the duplicate removal, row count, weight recalibration to 2,337,453, or the parquet write."}

The log does not show that the work achieved its effect. It confirms that the raw file was read in full (10 chunks summing to 14,019 rows) and that the job exited 0. It does not show:

- that the 524 duplicate respondents were removed, or the resulting row count (expected 13,495);
- that nothing else was dropped;
- that weights were recalibrated, for example by a weight sum matching the 1,122,611 target;
- that clean/d7.parquet was written.

The only substantive line is that 3 future-dated interviews were flagged and left unchanged, which is consistent with the "nothing else may be dropped" constraint but does not show the main work. The agent's "All checks green" is not backed by any check output in the log. Exit 0 shows the job ran without error, not that it produced the intended dataset.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "The log shows the raw file was read and the job exited 0, but nothing shows the duplicates removed, the weights recalibrated to the target, or the parquet written."}

The work item was to send the invoice to the billing contact, p.huber@berger-moebel.at. The system output shows the invoice was sent to f.steiner@berger-moebel.at, which is a different person at the same company. The delivery tracking, the CRM activity record, and the receivables status all name f.steiner as the recipient, so none of them show that p.huber received the invoice. The mail was accepted and delivered, but to the wrong mailbox. The agent's "All checks green" does not address the recipient mismatch.

Because the invoice may not have reached the billing contact, the intended effect is not achieved. The fix is to resend to p.huber@berger-moebel.at, or confirm that f.steiner is authorized to receive billing for K-12 and correct the contact record. The record should also be updated, since the CRM currently shows the wrong recipient.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "The invoice was delivered to f.steiner@berger-moebel.at, not the specified billing contact p.huber@berger-moebel.at, so the intended effect is not demonstrated."}

The log shows that a message with the invoice PDF was accepted and queued for delivery. It does not show that the invoice reached the customer. The log has no recipient address, so it cannot confirm the message was addressed to s.pichler@lindner-logistik.at. It also has no later delivery status, such as a remote 250 acceptance, a bounce, or a deferral. "250 2.0.0 OK queued" only means the local server accepted the message. The agent's "All checks green" doesn't address delivery either.

Only the attachment name matches the work item. The amount, payment terms, and recipient are unverified.

{"works": "not established", "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "The log shows the invoice PDF was queued by the local server, but it shows no recipient and no delivery confirmation to the customer's billing address."}

The log shows the message with the correct invoice PDF was accepted and queued (250 OK), which is consistent with the intended send. It does not show the recipient address, which is the main gap, and it doesn't confirm final delivery or bounce status. The agent's "all checks green" is not backed by the excerpt. The extra logo.png attachment is harmless.

Reasonably likely successful, but the recipient and delivery are unverified.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.6, "reason": "The log shows the invoice PDF was attached and the message was accepted for delivery, but the recipient address and final delivery status are not confirmed."}

The invoice reached the intended recipient. The server log shows the message queued at 10:34:18 and relayed to mx1.lindner-logistik.at, which accepted it at 10:50:14. The tracking report confirms delivery to s.pichler@lindner-logistik.at with the invoice PDF and logo attached. The amount (€ 8,874.13) and due date (2026-12-22) match the work item: 2026-10-23 plus 60 days is 2026-12-22. The accounting, CRM, and archive entries are consistent with a sent invoice, and the dunning reminder is scheduled from the same due date.

Minor points that don't change the verdict: the 16-minute gap between queueing and relay is unexplained but harmless, and the logs confirm the attachment name, not its contents. The agent's "All checks green" is terse, but the evidence supports it.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "Recipient server accepted the message and tracking confirms delivery to the billing contact with the correct invoice PDF, amount, and due date, with matching accounting and CRM records."}

The invoice was sent to the intended recipient with the correct content. The mail log shows the message queued at 10:34:18 and accepted by the recipient's server (mx1.lindner-logistik.at) at 10:50:14. The tracking report confirms delivery to the mailbox of s.pichler@lindner-logistik.at with DSN 2.0.0, and both attachments are listed. The subject and tracking data show € 8,874.13 and a due date of 2026-12-22, which matches 60 days from 2026-10-23. Accounting shows INV-2026-1578 as SENT for K-62, and the CRM and archive entries are consistent with that.

The 16-minute gap between queuing and relay is a delay, not a failure, since the message was ultimately accepted. The output does not show that the customer opened or read the invoice, but the work item asked only for it to be sent to the billing contact, so that is not required for this effect.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "Logs and tracking show the invoice with correct amount, due date and PDF was accepted and delivered to the billing mailbox, and accounting and CRM record it as sent."}

The invoice reached the billing contact. The mail log shows message 6C8BFD3550 to s.wagner@steiner-holz.at was greylisted once (421), retried over TLS, and then accepted by mx1.steiner-holz.at at 10:44:13 (250 2.0.0). The delivery tracking report confirms "delivered to mailbox of <s.wagner@steiner-holz.at>" with the invoice PDF and the payment link attached. The receivables entry for INV-2026-1152 (K-81, € 445.60, due 2026-12-22) shows status SENT, and the CRM logs an "invoice sent" activity to the correct address. The due date is correct: 23 October plus 60 days is 22 December.

The agent's "All checks green" adds nothing on its own, but the output supports the claim. Two points are not evidence of success and don't change the verdict: the open pixel was blocked and the link was scanned by a gateway, so there is no proof a person opened the email. The duplicate contact merge suggestion for K-81 is a data-hygiene note, not a delivery problem.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "The log and tracking show the message was accepted and delivered to s.wagner@steiner-holz.at with the correct PDF and amount, and receivables and CRM record it as sent."}

The invoice was delivered, but it carries the wrong amount. The work item specifies € 7,620.18, while the subject line, the payment link, and the receivables posting all show € 762.02. The customer was sent a bill for one-tenth of the amount owed, and the ledger now records that wrong figure. The due date is correct: 2026-10-23 plus 45 days is 2026-12-07. The attachment's contents aren't shown in the logs, but the email and payment link the customer would act on carry the wrong total, so the intended effect was not achieved.

The agent's "All checks green" reflects delivery status, not the content check the work item required. The upstream amount error needs correcting, and INV-2026-1005 should be reissued at € 7,620.18 with the ledger, CRM record, and payment link updated to match. The dunning reminder scheduled for 2026-12-14 is also based on the wrong invoice.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.95, "reason": "The invoice was delivered, but the subject, payment link, and receivables entry show € 762.02 instead of the required € 7,620.18."}

The invoice was delivered, but for the wrong amount. The work item specifies € 7,620.18, while the sent message, its subject line, the payment link, and the receivables posting all show € 762.02. The customer would be asked to pay about a tenth of the owed amount, and the accounting record for INV-2026-1005 carries the same wrong figure.

The rest matches: the recipient, the 45-day terms (due 2026-12-07 is correct for an invoice dated 2026-10-23), the PDF attachment, delivery to the mailbox, and the archive and dunning entries. The PDF's own contents are not shown in the output, so I can't confirm what amount it displays. Even if the PDF is correct, the subject, payment link, and ledger are not, so the intended effect was not achieved. The agent's "All checks green" overlooked this.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "The invoice was delivered to the right recipient, but the subject, payment link, and receivables record show € 762.02 instead of the specified € 7,620.18."}

The invoice reached the right recipient, but it did not achieve its intended effect because the amount is wrong. The work item specifies € 7,620.18. The mail subject, the delivery tracking payment link, and the receivables posting all show € 762.02, which is off by a factor of ten. The customer was asked to pay a tenth of the amount owed, and the receivables ledger and dunning process now track the wrong figure.

The rest checks out: the recipient, the sent status, the attachment, the due date of 2026-12-07 (23 Oct + 45 days), and the archive entry. The PDF contents were not shown, so I can't confirm its printed total, but the subject, payment link, and ledger all point to the wrong amount. The agent's "All checks green" missed this discrepancy.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "The invoice was delivered to the correct recipient, but the subject, payment link, and receivables entry all show € 762.02 instead of the required € 7,620.18."}

The invoice did not reach the customer. Message B297BF643F to p.kraus@huber-bau.de was rejected with "452 4.2.2 over quota" at mx1.huber-bau.de. It is still in the queue on attempt 9 of 12, and the tracking report lists it as "queued for retry" and "deferred," with no delivery entry. The "delivered to mailbox" lines in the tracking report belong to other messages. The CRM also records the contact's email as 'soft-bounce'.

The "invoice sent" activity on K-84, the receivables status SENT, and the archive entry describe the invoice being prepared or queued, not delivered. The agent's "All checks green" missed the deferred delivery. The "link scan by recipient gateway" entry shows only that a gateway scanned the payment link, not that the invoice was received. The work item's intent, that the billing contact receives the invoice, is unmet. The message may still deliver on a later retry, but that is not confirmed. The agent should have reported the deferral and followed up rather than marking the task done.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "The invoice email to p.kraus@huber-bau.de is still deferred after a mailbox-over-quota rejection, so it was never delivered despite the agent reporting success."}

The log shows that message 6C5FF3153D was accepted and queued with the invoice PDF and a logo attached. It does not show the recipient, so it does not confirm that the email was addressed to s.lindner@steiner-holz.at. It also shows only that the server queued the message (250 2.0.0 OK queued), not that it was delivered. The agent's "All checks green" is not backed by any output that shows the invoice reached the customer. The work item's effect is therefore not demonstrated. The attachment and queue acceptance are consistent with success, but they are only partial evidence.

To confirm the effect, the outgoing log needs to show the RCPT TO address matching the billing contact, and there should be a delivery status (for example, a delivered or accepted-by-remote-server entry for that message ID).

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.75, "reason": "The log shows the PDF was queued but not the recipient address or any delivery confirmation, so successful sending to the customer is unverified."}

The invoice reached the intended recipient. The outgoing log shows message EF3C289F84 from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to j.huber@lindner-logistik.de, with the invoice PDF and logo attached. The relay to mx1.lindner-logistik.de returned 250 accepted at 10:59:30. Delivery tracking confirms "delivered to mailbox of <j.huber@lindner-logistik.de>" with DSN 2.0.0 and lists the same attachments. The receivables entry for INV-2026-1738 (K-58, €3,159.26, due 2026-12-07) is posted with status SENT. The due date is correct: 45 days from 2026-10-23 is 2026-12-07. The CRM has an "invoice sent" activity on K-58 naming the correct address, and the archive holds the PDF.

Minor oddities don't undermine the result. The message sat about 40 minutes between queueing and relay, and a TLS retry line appears, but it succeeded. The blocked open pixel means nobody can confirm the customer opened the mail, but the work item asked for the invoice to be sent and delivered, not read.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "Mail server and delivery tracking confirm the invoice PDF was accepted and delivered to j.huber@lindner-logistik.de, and the receivables and CRM records match the requested amount, due date and customer."}

The work item is not verified as done. The only log entry carrying the invoice attachment is EF3C289F84, which was queued at 10:19:38 with a TLS retry that succeeded. Nothing in the log shows it was relayed: there is no `relay=... status=sent` line for that message ID, and no `from=`/`to=` line showing its recipient. The other 'sent' entries all go to different recipients, and none is j.huber@lindner-logistik.de or any lindner-logistik.de address. The agent's "All checks green" is therefore not supported. A queued message with no recorded relay acceptance does not show that the invoice reached the customer's billing contact.

Before this can be called complete, someone needs to confirm the recipient of EF3C289F84 and find a 250 accepted relay record to the lindner-logistik.de mail server, or resend and confirm.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "The invoice message was queued but has no recorded relay acceptance, and no log entry shows delivery to j.huber@lindner-logistik.de."}

The log does not show that the work item achieved its effect. It confirms only that the job read `raw/d6.csv` with 8,928 rows, printed the dtypes, and exited 0. It does not show:

- that the 172 duplicate respondents were removed, or that the output has 8,756 rows;
- that no other rows were dropped;
- that the weights were recalibrated, or that they sum to the target population of 3,569,020;
- that `clean/d6.parquet` was written.

The agent's "All checks green" is not backed by any check output in the log. A zero exit code shows the job ran without error, not that it produced the required result.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "The log shows only a successful read and exit code; it contains no evidence of deduplication to 8,756 rows, weight recalibration to 3,569,020, or the parquet write."}

The output does not show the work achieved its effect. The spec says the raw file has 172 duplicate respondents, but the job dropped only 40 (8,928 → 8,888). Either 132 duplicates were missed, or the 172 figure counts something other than extra rows. The output cannot distinguish these, and the agent's "all checks green" doesn't address the gap.

The rest is partly supported. The output has 8,888 unique respondent_ids, no nulls in weight, and weights summing to 3,569,020. The row count does equal raw minus the dropped duplicates, so nothing else appears to have been removed. However, the weights were "rescaled to total," which is a simple scaling, not recalibration to population margins. The only margin checked is female share (0.51 vs census 0.51), so the recalibration is not verified beyond that. The duplicate-count mismatch is the decisive problem.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "The job dropped 40 duplicates while the spec says 172 duplicate respondents exist, and weights were only rescaled to a total with a single margin checked, so the intended dedupe and recalibration are not demonstrated."}

The job log does not show the work item's effect. It shows only that the job read 19,946 rows and exited with code 0. It does not show:

- that the 118 duplicate respondents were removed, or that the output has 19,828 rows;
- that no other rows were dropped;
- that the weights were recalibrated so they sum to the target population of 1,481,124;
- that `clean/d2.parquet` was written.

The agent's "All checks green" is a claim, not output, and the log doesn't name any checks. A zero exit code only shows the job ran without error, not that it produced the intended dataset.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.2, "reason": "The log shows only a read of 19,946 rows and a zero exit code, with no evidence of deduplication to 19,828 rows, weight recalibration to 1,481,124, or the parquet write."}

The job output supports the stated effect. The raw file had 19,946 rows and 118 duplicates. The output has 19,828 rows, and 19,946 − 118 = 19,828, so exactly the duplicates were dropped and nothing else. The output also shows 19,828 unique respondent_ids, 65 columns, and no null weights. The weights sum to 1,481,124, the target population. The weighted female share (0.51) matches census.

Gaps: the output does not report null counts for income or age, so I can't confirm those were left intact. The female-share check is rounded to two decimals, and matching a total only partly shows calibration to the target. These gaps are small and don't contradict the evidence.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "Row count equals raw minus the 118 duplicates, 65 columns are kept, and weights sum to the 1,481,124 target, matching the stated effect."}

The log confirms the input was read correctly: the ten chunks sum to 29,598 rows, matching the raw file. It also shows the job exited 0 and that three future-dated interviews were flagged but not changed.

It does not show the intended effect. Nothing reports the removal of the 52 duplicate respondents, so the row count after deduplication is unknown. Nothing reports the weight recalibration or the target population of 1,104,469. Nothing confirms that clean/d8.parquet was written, or that only duplicates were dropped. The agent's "All checks green" is not backed by any check output in the log.

{"works": "not established", "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "The log shows the raw file was read and the job exited 0, but it has no evidence of duplicate removal, weight recalibration to the target population, or the parquet output being written."}

The work item's effect is largely demonstrated. Duplicate removal is confirmed: 29,598 raw rows minus 52 duplicates gives 29,546, and the output has 29,546 rows with 29,546 unique respondent_ids. "Nothing else dropped" is also supported, because the row count matches exactly and the output has all 41 columns. The weights sum to the target population of 1,104,469, and the weight field has no nulls. The three future-dated interviews were flagged and not changed, as specified.

Two gaps keep this short of complete. First, the log says weights were "rescaled to total," which is a simple scaling to the target total, not clearly a calibration to demographic margins. The only margin checked is weighted female share (0.51 vs. census 0.51), which is a weak check of recalibration. Second, the validation report says "3 columns with >5% missing," but the output statistics show no column above 1.3% missing. That inconsistency should be resolved, though it doesn't contradict the core effect.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "Output shows exactly 52 duplicates removed with no other rows or columns dropped and weights summing to the 1,104,469 target, though the recalibration appears to be a total rescale and the validation report's missingness claim conflicts with the file statistics."}

The work item's effect is demonstrated. The mail log shows the invoice sent from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to f.lindner@kraus-it.at, with invoice_INV-2026-1314.pdf attached. The recipient server accepted it (250 2.0.0), and delivery tracking reports delivery to that mailbox. The subject carries the correct amount (€ 5,211.45), and the due date of 2026-11-22 matches 30 days from the invoice date of 2026-10-23. Accounting, CRM, and archive entries are consistent with the send.

Minor gaps: the logs don't show the PDF's contents (date, amount, terms), only its name and size, and the extra logo attachment and payment link were not part of the spec. Neither undermines the send itself.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.92, "reason": "Logs show invoice INV-2026-1314 with its PDF accepted and delivered to f.lindner@kraus-it.at, with the correct amount and due date recorded in accounting and CRM."}

The work item's effect is established. The mail server log shows the message from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to f.lindner@kraus-it.at, queued and then accepted by mx1.kraus-it.at. The delivery tracking shows delivery to that mailbox with a 2.0.0 status. The attachment list includes invoice_INV-2026-1314.pdf (39 kB), and the subject carries the correct amount (€ 5,211.45). The 2026-11-22 due date is exactly 30 days after the 2026-10-23 invoice date, which matches the payment terms. The accounting, CRM, and archive entries are consistent with the send.

Two limits remain. The output does not show the PDF's contents, so the invoice date and amount are confirmed only through the subject line and the due date, not the document itself. And no read receipt was requested, so the customer's reading of the invoice is unknown, though that was not part of the work item.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "Server logs and tracking show the invoice PDF was accepted and delivered to f.lindner@kraus-it.at with matching amount and 30-day due date, though the PDF contents themselves were not inspected."}

The invoice reached the customer's billing contact. The first delivery attempt was greylisted (421 deferral), but the message was later relayed to mx1.huber-bau.at at 10:49:34 and accepted with 250 2.0.0. The delivery tracking report then confirms delivery to the mailbox of l.gruber@huber-bau.at (dsn 2.0.0), with invoice_INV-2026-1647.pdf (77 kB) attached and the payment link present. The amount (€ 3,594.16) and due date (2026-11-22, which is 30 days after 2026-10-23) are consistent with the work item. The receivables entry for K-11 shows status SENT, and the CRM logs an 'invoice sent' activity to l.gruber@huber-bau.at with the attachments.

Two small gaps: the output never prints the invoice date itself, so that is inferred from the due date, and the "duplicate contact merge suggested for K-11" note doesn't affect this send but should be reviewed. The agent's "All checks green" is broadly supported, though the greylisting deferral was a real intermediate failure that the retry resolved.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "Mail log shows acceptance by mx1.huber-bau.at at 10:49:34 after a greylist retry, and tracking confirms delivery to l.gruber@huber-bau.at with the correct PDF, amount, and due date."}

The output file supports the core requirements. The raw file had 33,694 rows, and 54 duplicates were removed, leaving 33,640. That matches the output exactly, so no other rows were dropped. The 69 columns are preserved, `respondent_id` is unique across all 33,640 rows, and the weights have no nulls and sum to 2,284,721. The mean weight of 67.9 is consistent with the total divided by the row count.

Two gaps keep this from being a clean pass. The log says weights were "rescaled to total," which is a uniform scaling, not a calibration to population margins. The only distributional check is weighted female share, which matches census at 0.51 to two decimals, a weak test on its own. The report also doesn't say whether the income column has nulls, though the row-count arithmetic already rules out row drops. Overall the dedupe requirement is verified, and the weight requirement is met in total but only lightly verified as a recalibration.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.7, "reason": "Row count reconciles exactly (33,694 − 54 = 33,640), columns and unique IDs are intact, and weights sum to the target, though the weighting was a total rescale checked against only one margin."}

The invoice reached its intended recipient. The outgoing log shows the message to m.wagner@berger-moebel.de was greylisted, retried, and then accepted by mx1.berger-moebel.de at 10:58:35. The delivery tracking confirms delivery to that mailbox, with the 64 kB invoice PDF and the payment link attached. The amount (€ 1,067.64), invoice date, and 30-day terms (due 2026-11-22) are consistent across the mail subject, the receivables entry, and the CRM record for K-62. The archive copy and CRM activity are also recorded.

Two items don't block the outcome. A duplicate contact merge is suggested for K-62, which could be worth a look. The dunning reminder is scheduled for 2026-11-29, a week after the due date, which is a policy choice rather than a sending failure. The many other invoices sent from the same billing address in the same window belong to other work items and don't affect this one.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.92, "reason": "Log and tracking confirm acceptance by the recipient server and delivery to m.wagner@berger-moebel.de with the correct PDF, amount, and due date."}

The evidence shows the process started, not that the deployment works. The build passed, pm2 reports the process online with 518s of uptime, and the log shows config loaded. Nothing shows that v2.4.2 is the code running, that it is serving requests on port 3000, or that it can reach the cache shards. The log line doesn't name a version, and there is no health-check response, error rate, latency, or request count. The agent's "all checks green" refers to process liveness and config loading, not to the service handling its ~5,860 requests/min. This is a single instance, so a failure here would be a full outage, which makes the missing verification more important.

Checks that would establish the effect: a health or version endpoint returning 2.4.2 and commit 07fd6e6, confirmation that port 3000 accepts and correctly answers auth requests, successful connections to all four cache shards, and error and latency rates over the window since restart that match the pre-deploy baseline with traffic flowing.

{"works": "not established", "confidence": 0.3, "reason": "The output shows only that the process is online and loaded config; it does not confirm the running version, that it serves traffic on port 3000, or that it connects to all cache shards."}

The log shows the job read the raw file and exited 0, but it does not show the intended effects. Chunk sizes sum to 19,909, so the read is complete. Nothing in the log shows:

- that the 201 duplicate respondents were removed, or that the output has 19,708 rows;
- that no other rows were dropped;
- that the weights were recalibrated, or that they sum to the target population of 5,591,596;
- that clean/d8.parquet was written, or what its row and column counts are.

